{{Shrine |name = Yasaka Shrine (八坂神社) |religious_affiliation = {{ill|Shinto|ja|神道|12=draft|12=draft}} |image = [[File:Hino Ysaka-jinja ac (1).jpg|280px]] |location = {{ill|Tokyo|ja|東京都|12=draft|12=draft}} {{ill|Hino City|ja|日野市|12=draft|12=draft}} Hino Honchō 3-14-12 |latitude_deg = 35 |latitude_min = 40 |latitude_sec = 44.8 |longitude_deg = 139 |longitude_min = 23 |longitude_sec = 44.6 |map = |deity = {{ill|Susanoo|ja|素盞嗚尊|12=draft|12=draft}} |type = |founded = {{ill|Ōnei|ja|応永|12=draft|12=draft}} 5 (1398) |main_shrine = |alias = |sacred_places = |festival = |rites = }}
'''Yasaka Shrine''' (八坂神社, yasaka jinja) is a Shinto shrine located at Hino Honchō 3-14-12 in {{ill|Hino City|ja|日野市|12=draft|12=draft}}, {{ill|Tokyo|ja|東京都|12=draft|12=draft}}. It is also known as '''Hino Yasaka Shrine''' (ひのやさかじんじゃ). Its former shrine rank was that of a village shrine, and it serves as the general guardian shrine (総鎮守) of Hino.
== History == [[File:Hino Ysaka-jinja ac (3).jpg|thumb|Main Hall]]
The shrine was founded in the 5th year of the Ōnei era (1398). In that same year, {{ill|Fumon-ji (Hino)|ja|普門寺 (日野市)|12=draft|12=draft}} was established by its founding monk, and as its guardian temple, the {{ill|Gozu Tennō Shrine|ja|牛頭天王社|12=draft|12=draft}} was founded—this being the origin of Yasaka Shrine. Later, in the first year of the Genki era (1570), the shrine was relocated to its present site.<ref name="散歩">Ryūtarō Akutagawa, ''A Walk Through the Historic Sites of Hachioji, Hino, Fuchū, Chōfu, Machida (Musashino-Tama Historic Guide ①)'', Gakuseisha, 1979, pp. 87–88</ref><ref name="観光">[http://shinsenhino.com/archives/spot/temple/050322014055.php Yasaka Shrine (commonly known as "Tenno-sama")] Hino City Tourism Association</ref>
In the 12th year of the Kansei era (1800), a main hall featuring exquisite carvings was constructed. In 1869 (the 2nd year of the Meiji era), due to the {{ill|Separation of Shinto and Buddhism|ja|神仏分離|12=draft|12=draft}}, the shrine was detached from Fumon-ji and the worship of {{ill|Gozu Tennō|ja|牛頭天王|1=Gozu Tennō|1=Gozu Tennō|12=draft|12=draft}} was repudiated. Consequently, the enshrined deity was changed from Gozu Tennō to {{ill|Susanoo|ja|素盞嗚尊|12=draft|12=draft}}, and the shrine was renamed "Yasaka Shrine".<ref name="観光"></ref>
On October 1, 1961 (Showa 36), the main hall was designated as a cultural property by Hino City.
== Cultural Properties == === Hino City Designated Cultural Properties === * Yasaka Shrine Main Hall – Designated on October 1, 1961<ref name="市">[https://www.city.hino.lg.jp/bunka/bunka/bunkazai/1003232.html List of Designated Cultural Properties] Hino City</ref>.
== Access == * A 2‑minute walk from Hino Station on the JR Chūō Main Line ({{ill|JR Hino Station|ja|日野駅 (東京都)|12=draft|12=draft}}).
== References == {{Footnotes}} {{Reflist}}
== Further Reading == {{Commonscat|Hino Yasaka-jinja}} * Ryūtarō Akutagawa, ''A Walk Through the Historic Sites of Hachioji, Hino, Fuchū, Chōfu, Machida (Musashino-Tama Historic Guide ①)'', Gakuseisha, 1979.
